###############################################################################
# 06 — The client portal: the complete surface an external principal may reach
#
# Four operations. All GET. Nothing here mutates anything, and there is no fifth
# endpoint hiding elsewhere in the spec.
#
# Use a token belonging to a CLIENT principal for these. Using an INTERNAL token
# works too — the permissions allow it — but it proves nothing about the
# boundary. The interesting run is the CLIENT one, and the interesting result is
# what comes back missing.
#
# NEVER COMMIT A REAL CREDENTIAL OR TOKEN TO THIS FILE.
###############################################################################

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8090

# A token for an external CLIENT principal with an ACTIVE client membership.
@clientAccessToken = REPLACE_WITH_A_CLIENT_PRINCIPAL_ACCESS_TOKEN

@projectId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000007
@taskId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000009


### Projects shared with me
# Only `cursor` and `limit` are accepted here. There is no `sort`, no
# `direction`, no `status` and no `department_id`, because an external principal
# must not learn that departments exist — see the probe at the bottom of this
# file.
#
# Correct response: 200 {items, next_cursor, has_more} where each item has
# exactly nine fields: id, code, name, description, status, start_date,
# target_date, completed_at, updated_at.
#
# Absent by construction, not by filtering: internal_note, created_by,
# manager_user_id, department_id, version. `ClientProject` is a separate type
# from `Project`, so those fields have no place in it to leak from.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/client-portal/projects?limit=25
Authorization: Bearer {{clientAccessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### One shared project
# Correct response: 200 with the same nine-field projection.
#
# A project that is NOT shared with one of your active client accounts answers
# 404, never 403 — the refusal must not confirm that the project exists.
# Visibility is derived per request from a live share joined to an ACTIVE
# membership joined to an ACTIVE client account; holding the UUID authorises
# nothing.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/client-portal/projects/{{projectId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{clientAccessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Client-visible tasks of a shared project
# Returns ONLY tasks explicitly marked `client_visible`. Sharing a project does
# not share its tasks; the flag is per task, defaults to false and is never
# inherited.
#
# Correct response: 200 with items carrying id, project_id, title, description,
# status, due_date, completed_at, updated_at. `internal_note`, `created_by`,
# assignee identities, `version` — and `client_visible` itself — are absent.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/client-portal/projects/{{projectId}}/tasks?limit=25
Authorization: Bearer {{clientAccessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### One client-visible task
# Correct response: 200 with the reduced projection.
# A task that is not client-visible, or whose project is not shared with you,
# answers 404.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/client-portal/tasks/{{taskId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{clientAccessToken}}
Accept: application/json


###############################################################################
# Boundary probes. These MUST fail. Run them with the CLIENT token.
###############################################################################

### A CLIENT reaching an internal route
# EXPECTED: 404, code RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND.
# Not 403: for an external principal a would-be 403 is converted to a 404, so
# that a refusal does not confirm the existence of anything internal. The
# refusal happens at the principal-envelope check, before any grant is even
# looked up, because `projects.read` has max_principal_type INTERNAL.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/projects
Authorization: Bearer {{clientAccessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### A CLIENT reading the internal projection of a project it CAN see in the portal
# EXPECTED: 404, code RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND — even though the same project is
# readable at /client-portal/projects/{id}. The portal projection is the only
# view an external principal has of it.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/projects/{{projectId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{clientAccessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### A CLIENT reading the user directory
# EXPECTED: 404, code RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users
Authorization: Bearer {{clientAccessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### A CLIENT trying to sort or filter the portal listing
# EXPECTED: 400, code VALIDATION_FAILED. The portal list query accepts `cursor`
# and `limit` and nothing else; unknown query parameters are rejected rather
# than ignored. Ignoring them is how a filter that should not exist quietly
# starts working after somebody widens the shared query struct.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/client-portal/projects?limit=25&sort=name&direction=asc&department_id=00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000004
Authorization: Bearer {{clientAccessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### A CLIENT trying to write through the portal
# EXPECTED: 405 from the router — there is no POST on this path in the route
# table, so it is not merely forbidden, it does not exist. The client portal is
# read-only and a test in routes.rs asserts every portal route is a GET.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/client-portal/projects
Authorization: Bearer {{clientAccessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "A project I invented"
}
